'''Tomohito''' ({{nihongo|Tomohito-shinnō|智仁親王}}; born 15 May 2002) is the Crown Prince of [[Okaiken]] and the heir apparent to the Wisteria Throne. He is the eldest son of the current Emperor [[Emperor Masahito|Masahito]] and Empress Michiko. Distinguished by his recent background in competitive collegiate athletics and physical sciences, Tomohito is widely viewed as the face of the "New Generation" of the Imperial House, embodying a blend of traditional stoicism and modern durability.

==Early life==
===Birth and succession===
Prince Tomohito was born at 09:12 JST in 2002 at the Okami Central Hospital in the capital city of [[Okami]]. At the time of his birth, Okaiken was governed as a republic, and he was born as a private citizen under the name Tomohito Honda. As the eldest son of Masahito and Michiko Honda, his birth occurred during the later years of the republican period, nearly a decade before the [[Jutsu Restoration|Jutsu Restoration of 2011]]. While he lacked formal royal status at birth, his lineage remained a point of significant public interest among those who favored a return to traditional governance. Upon the restoration of the monarchy in 2011, he was formally invested as the Crown Prince, centering him as the primary heir to the Wisteria Throne.

===Early childhood===
His personal name, Tomohito, was chosen to signify wise virtue or enduring intelligence, reflecting his parents' desire for him to possess a balanced and thoughtful character. Growing up in the republican era, his early years were relatively private compared to traditional imperial standards. He was raised alongside his younger sister, Kiko, and his younger brother, Teruhito, in an environment that prioritized academic discipline and physical activity. His childhood was marked by a reserved and observant nature; family accounts from this period often describe him as a child who was more interested in how things were built and operated than in the social spotlight, a trait that would later evolve into his scientific and industrial focus.

===Education at Gakushūin===
Tomohito attended the [[Gakushūin]] School system, which maintained its prestige during the republican period as a premier institution for the nation's elite. His education was defined by a functional approach to learning, where he was encouraged to master both classical Sanese literature and the foundations of the physical sciences. Despite the lack of formal titles during his early schooling, he was noted for a pragmatic and somewhat blunt personality. He often favored direct, hands-on learning over abstract theory, a characteristic that set him apart from the more academic inclinations of his peers and signaled his future alignment with the "Forge State" ideology promoted by his father.

During his high school years at Gakushūin, which coincided with the early years of the Jutsu Restoration, Tomohito sought to maintain the grounded perspective of his republican upbringing. He became a prominent member of the school's rugby team, famously competing under the name Tomo Honda to avoid the intensified media scrutiny surrounding the newly restored throne. This period of his life, often called his secret years, was essential in tempering his character through physical struggle and camaraderie. It was on the rugby pitch and in the classrooms of Gakushūin that he solidified his "Iron" identity.

=== Education at Okami Teikoku University ===
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito hanging out.png|thumb|right|250px|Crown Prince with his friends from [[Okami Imperial University]].]]
In April 2021, Tomohito enrolled in [[Okami Teikoku University]], breaking with the recent imperial tradition of studying law or the humanities at Gakushūin University. He entered the Faculty of Science, majoring in [[Chemistry]] with a specialized focus on industrial metallurgy. His decision to attend a national university rather than a private institution was seen as a deliberate alignment with the "Forge State" ideology, emphasizing meritocracy and technical utility over aristocratic prestige.

During his undergraduate studies, the Prince was noted for his intense focus on the physical sciences. He frequently conducted fieldwork at the heavy industrial zones in the [[Nankō Port Town]] district, often unaccompanied by security detail to better integrate with the workforce. His academic circle during this time included his close confidants, Ran Tsubonouchi and Naoto Ishimura, who were fellow students and rugby athletes at the university. Professors described him as a "diligent and practical" student who preferred laboratory experimentation to theoretical lectures.

He graduated in March 2025 with a Bachelor of Science degree. His senior thesis, titled ''Thermal Resilience of High-Carbon Alloys in Marine Environments'', received high distinction for its practical application to Okaiken's shipbuilding industry. The research was later published in the ''Journal of the Okaiken Chemical Society'', and shortly after graduation, he accepted an honorary patronage with the [[Okaiken Chemical Society]]. This academic background has allowed him to engage deeply with the nation's vital steel and manufacturing sectors, legitimizing his role as the "Iron Prince."

==Athletic career==
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito Roster Photo.png|thumb|left|250px|The crown prince athlete's roster profile.]]
The Crown Prince is best known to the general public for his semi-professional rugby career. During his university years from 2021 to 2024, he joined the Okami Teikoku University Rugby Football Club, playing primarily as an openside flanker (number 7). He refused special security details nor special exemptions on the field, insisting on full-contact participation. He earned a reputation as a "jackal" for his ability to steal the ball at the breakdown and famously played through a broken nose during the 2023 University Championship semi-finals against rival Keio University.

[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito rugby play.png|thumb|right|300px|The crown prince playing rugby for the Okaiken Teikoku University.]]
In his senior year (2024), he served as the team captain, leading the university to a second-place finish in the National University Rugby Championship. Although he received offers to trial with the Okami Steelers in the Okaiken Rugby League One, his imperial duties necessitated his retirement from competitive league play in early 2025. He remains an active practitioner of the sport and frequently joins charity matches.

