==Crown Prince==
[[File:Ceremony for Proclamation of Crown Prince 2025.png|thumb|left|250px|Tomohito at his Ceremony for Proclamation of Crown Prince (''Rikkōshi-Senmei-no-gi'') in 2025.]]
Following his graduation from university, Tomohito was formally invested as Crown Prince ({{nihongo|Kōtaishi|皇太子}}) in a ceremony held at the Imperial Palace on 15 April 2025 called the ''Rikkōshi-Senmei-no-gi''. The event was noted for its blend of archaic court ritual and modern pragmatism; while Tomohito wore the traditional saffron-colored robes for the religious rites, he famously requested that the subsequent state banquet be replaced with a tour of the Okami Minami Industrial District to highlight the nation's manufacturing sector.

Tomohito currently resides in the Togu Palace in the Akasaka Estate. Reports from the Imperial Household Agency indicate that the Prince has significantly modified the residence, converting the former reception hall into a state-of-the-art gymnasium and a mechanical workshop where he engages in automotive restoration. These modifications reflect his philosophy that the monarchy must be a site of "active creation" rather than passive existence.

==Official duties==
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito.png|thumb|left|200px|A photo of the crown prince wearing imperial regalia]]
Upon reaching his majority in 2022, Tomohito began undertaking limited official duties, though his primary focus remained on his scientific studies until 2025. Since his formal investiture, he has assumed a full schedule of public engagements, characterized by a distinct focus on the industrial, scientific, and youth sectors. His approach to these duties is often described by political analysts as "Technocratic Royalty," as he frequently engages with engineers and policy-makers on a technical level rather than purely ceremonial one.

=== The "Iron Body" Initiative ===
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito Juddo.png|thumb|right|250px|Crown Prince Tomohito holds a black belt in Judo and frequently participates in public sparring matches.]]
A central pillar of Tomohito's public work is the {{Nihongo|Iron Body initiative|鉄身運動|Tesshin-undō}}, a public health campaign launched in late 2025. Arguing that physical resilience is a prerequisite for national endurance, the Prince encourages youth citizens to engage in weightlifting, rugby, and traditional martial arts. He frequently visits high schools and dojos, often dispensing with protocol to participate in full-contact sparring matches or scrum drills with students.

His distinct physical appearance—muscular and bearded—serves as the visual branding for this initiative. His beard, grown during his university years, is relatively uncommon among the clean-shaven Sanese aristocracy. Cultural commentators have cited it as a deliberate visual nod to the warrior-emperors of the Sengoku period, specifically referencing the portraits of his ancestor, Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi, thereby linking modern physical fitness with the nation's martial history.

=== National Iron Body Initiative ===
In January 2026, the program was elevated to a state-sponsored framework known as the {{nihongo|National Iron Body Initiative|国家鉄身計画|
Kokka Tesshin Keikaku}}. To commemorate the launch, the Prince organized a high-profile rugby exhibition at the Okami National Stadium, drawing a capacity crowd of over 60,000 spectators. The event featured the Okaiken Teikoku Rugby Exhibition—a team comprised of former university teammates and active-duty military personnel—competing against a select side from the Okaiken Rugby League One.

The Prince participated in the full duration of the match at his traditional position of flanker. He was supported on the pitch by his inner circle, including Ran Tsubonouchi, who anchored the scrum, and Naoto Ishimura, whose explosive performance as a winger was highlighted by national media. Fusei Hineno was credited with the team's grappling-based conditioning program, which emphasized the functional power required for the "Forge State" ideal.

The initiative has expanded into a nationwide network of community centers and corporate gymnasiums. It mandates that participating institutions provide subsidized access to strength training equipment and traditional martial arts instruction. The Prince has described the movement as a biological foundation for national sovereignty, asserting that a resilient citizenry is the ultimate defense against both physical and digital stagnation. Following the Okami exhibition, the initiative's "Warrior-Industrial" aesthetic has seen a significant surge in popularity among young adults, leading to an increase in enrollment at local dojos and rugby clubs across the nation.

===Scientific and industrial patronage===
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito 2.png|thumb|left|200px|An [[Sanese Official Portraiture|official portrait]] of the crown prince in formal clothing.]]
Drawing on his degree in chemistry, Tomohito serves as the honorary president of the Okaiken Chemical Society. Unlike previous royal patrons who served as figureheads, Tomohito actively presides over the annual awards for industrial innovation, often questioning recipients on the chemical mechanics of their inventions. He is a vocal advocate for the integration of sustainable chemical processes in Okaiken's heavy industry, arguing that environmental resilience is a key component of national security.

He also holds the patronage of the Sanese Institute of Metallurgy. His tours of steelworks and foundries are highly publicized events; he is known to inspect safety standards personally and has championed the adoption of "Green Steel" hydrogen-reduction technologies to ensure Okaiken remains a competitive exporter in a decarbonizing global market.

===Diplomacy===
Tomohito’s diplomatic portfolio is distinct from the "Soft Power" cultural diplomacy practiced by his younger brother, [[Prince Teruhito]]. Instead, Tomohito engages in "Hard Industry" diplomacy. He has represented the Emperor at international science expos and trade summits, specifically advocating for Sanese heavy machinery and shipbuilding exports. Foreign delegations have noted his straightforward, business-like demeanor.

==Public image==
Tomohito is noted for his physical stature and distinct appearance, which departs significantly from the polished, clean-shaven aesthetic typical of modern Sanese aristocracy. Standing at 186&nbsp;cm (6&nbsp;ft 1 in) and maintaining a muscular build from his rugby training, he is often referred to by the domestic press as the  {{nihongo|"Iron Prince"|鉄の皇子|Tetsu-no-Miko}}.

His decision to grow and maintain a full beard during his university years generated significant media commentary. While initially criticized by conservative elements within the [[Imperial Household Agency]] as "unrefined," the style has resonated with the public as a deliberate homage to the martial portraits of the Sengoku-era warlords, particularly [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]]. Cultural critics have noted that Tomohito's visual presentation serves to reinforce the "Forge State" identity, projecting an image of masculine endurance and raw strength rather than courtly elegance.

==Personal interests==
[[File:Crown Prince Tomohito workout.png|thumb|right|175px|The Crown Prince working out.]]
Tomohito is an avid enthusiast of physical culture and nutrition. Drawing on his background in chemistry, he takes a scientific approach to training, personally designing his own hypertrophy and strength-conditioning programs. He has authored several anonymous articles in Sanese fitness journals discussing the biochemistry of muscle recovery.

Outside of athletics, he has a keen interest in mechanical engineering and automotive restoration. He reportedly spends his private time at the Togu Palace garageworks, restoring vintage Sanese utility vehicles and engines. This hobby aligns with the Imperial family's private tradition of mechanical competence, established by his grandfather.

